# UI Styling Rules

## Styling Approach

- Use Tailwind utility classes for styling.
- Prefer existing design tokens and utility patterns already present in the project.
- Use `cn()` from `@lib/utils.ts` for conditional class merging.
- Prefer shared variant helpers such as `cva` when a component supports multiple style variants.

## Design System Usage

- Reuse components from `@/components/ui` before introducing new primitives.
- Keep component styling consistent with the existing Shadcn-based system.
- Follow the project's CSS variable tokens and semantic utility classes when available.

## Responsive Design

- Build mobile-first layouts.
- Add breakpoint classes only where the layout actually changes.
- Keep spacing, typography, and density consistent with nearby screens.

## Accessibility

- Prefer semantic HTML elements.
- Ensure buttons, links, dialogs, inputs, and menus remain keyboard accessible.
- Add accessible labels or `aria-*` attributes when the UI would otherwise be ambiguous.
- Do not rely on color alone to communicate important state.

## Icons And Interaction

- Use `lucide-react` for icons unless an existing pattern already dictates something else.
- Keep hover, focus, and disabled states visible and consistent.
- Prefer readable UI over over-designed animation or one-off visual behavior.

## Prefer

- `className={cn(...)}`
- Existing `Button`, `Card`, `Dialog`, `Tabs`, `Badge`, and `Input` components
- Variant-driven styling for reusable components

## Avoid

- Inline style objects for normal UI work
- CSS modules or one-off styling systems unless the user asks for them
- Recreating design tokens locally when the project already has them

## Reference Files

- `@lib/utils.ts`
- `@components/ui/button.tsx`
- `@app/globals.css`
